;;; A pair contradiction's core names only its positive side, where that side
;;; is a fact root derives — M1e2 **W21**, register row
;;; `W21/pair-core-root-derived` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by
;;; S1e2.2.21 (2026-11-05), the witness's mechanism one derivation deeper.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Root derives `(p a)` from `(seed a)` by `mk`. One candidate, `(q a)`:
;;; committed, it derives `(not (p a))` by `neg`, a pair whose positive side
;;; root derives and whose negative side the commitment derives.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is {∅}, and N is {(q a)}. The pair rests on `(p a)`, which rests on
;;; `(seed a)`, and on `(not (p a))`, which rests on `(q a)`: the core is
;;; `(q a) (seed a)`, which names the commitment. `(seed a)` alone is
;;; root's, and root is alive.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R as above, exit 0; and N's core `(seed a)` alone, refuted by `mk` and
;;; `neg`, on every surface that reports it (`ein solve FILE --dump-answer
;;; DIR` at `cdcc3994`, release and debug): the positive side's frontier.

(relation seed T) (relation p T) (relation q T)

(seed a)

(rule mk () :match (seed ?x) :assert (p ?x))

(hrule g () :match (seed ?x) :assert (q ?x))

(rule neg () :match (q ?x) :assert (not (p ?x)))
